{
  "dom_id": "step-q156057",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4y-9}{15}<\\frac{y}{3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{4y-9}{15}) & <15\\cdot (\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$",
